            model:
              'One major reason medieval treatments focused on humoural balance was the universal belief in the Four Humours. Hippocrates and Galen taught that the body was made of four humours (blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile) and that illness was caused when these humours became unbalanced. Treatments were designed to remove whichever humour was in excess, using bloodletting (phlebotomy) with leeches or cuts, and purging with herbal laxatives. Because patients visibly lost bodily fluids during these treatments, both doctors and patients believed that the excess humours causing the illness were being successfully expelled. Therefore, treatments focused on humoural balance because evacuative therapies provided visible physical proof that the disease was leaving the body.<br><br>Furthermore, preventive health advice was structured entirely around keeping the humours in equilibrium, as shown by the Regimen Sanitatis. The Regimen Sanitatis (Rule of Health) was a widely used medieval health guide that advised people to regulate their diet, sleep, and exercise to prevent humoural imbalance. Wealthy people consulted physicians to check their personal humoural complexion and astrological birth charts, adapting their lifestyle (such as avoiding cold foods in winter) to keep their humours balanced. Therefore, treatments focused on humoural balance because preventing disease was seen as a daily routine of maintaining internal bodily harmony.<br><br>Finally, treatments focused on the humours because there was no alternative scientific understanding of what caused disease. Without microscopes, people had no knowledge of bacteria, viruses, or cellular biology. Since the Catholic Church strictly enforced Galen’s teachings and banned independent experimentation, physicians were never taught any rival theories of medicine. Without any awareness of germs or modern medicines, humoural theory remained the only rational explanation for sickness available to medieval physicians. Therefore, treatments focused on humoural balance because physicians had no other scientific framework to explain or treat illness.',
