            model:
              "William Harvey's discovery was a major turning point primarily because he used mathematical calculations to disprove Galen's 1,400-year-old ideas about blood. Galen taught that the liver constantly manufactured new blood from food, which was then burned up by bodily tissues as fuel. In De Motu Cordis (1628), Harvey calculated that the heart pumped approximately 540 pints of blood per hour, which was three times the weight of an average human body. This mathematical proof showed it was physically impossible for the liver to produce or the body to burn that amount of blood, proving that the same blood must circulate repeatedly in a closed loop. Therefore, Harvey's discovery was a turning point because mathematics definitively proved Galen's theory of blood consumption was completely wrong.<br><br>Furthermore, Harvey's discovery was a turning point because he proved his theories through repeatable scientific experiments. Harvey studied one-way valves in veins and carried out famous arm tourniquet experiments on living humans. By pushing blood down a vein away from the heart, he showed the vein stayed empty, proving that vein valves only permit blood to flow one way—back toward the heart. By publishing these experiments in De Motu Cordis, Harvey established experimental physiology as a scientific discipline, showing doctors that theories must be tested through practical demonstrations. Therefore, Harvey was a turning point because he established the modern scientific method of using physical experiments to prove how the human body works.<br><br>However, Harvey's discovery was only a partial turning point because it had very little immediate impact on treating living patients. Knowing that blood circulated did not cure illnesses, and attempts at blood transfusions failed and killed patients because blood groups were unknown. Crucially, despite Harvey proving that the body had a fixed amount of blood, doctors (including Harvey himself) continued to prescribe bloodletting for another 200 years because they lacked alternative treatments. Consequently, while Harvey transformed anatomical understanding, his discoveries could not be used to save lives until the 19th and 20th centuries. Therefore, Harvey was a turning point in scientific understanding and anatomy, but had almost no practical effect on curing sick patients at the time.",
